---
/**
 * The privacy page.
 *
 * This site had none, which was the wrong gap to leave open: Cupertino hands an
 * agent your mail, your messages and your contacts. Every other app in the
 * portfolio publishes a policy while holding far less.
 *
 * The honest story here is not "we collect nothing" — that is true and it is not
 * the interesting part. It is that **Cupertino makes no network calls of its own,
 * and hands your data to whatever agent you connected it to**, which may be a
 * cloud model. Anyone deciding whether to install this needs that sentence more
 * than they need a list of what we do not do.
 */
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import { APP_NAME, REPO_URL, SITE_URL } from "../config";

const updated = "30 August 2026";
---

<Layout
  title={`Privacy — ${APP_NAME}`}
  description={`How ${APP_NAME} handles your mail, messages, contacts and calendar: it makes no network calls of its own, and hands data only to the agent you connected.`}
>
  <Nav />
  <main class="wrap mx-auto max-w-[72ch] px-6 py-20 md:py-28">
    <div class="mono text-accent">sec · privacy</div>
    <h1 class="mt-3 font-display text-[clamp(34px,5vw,52px)] leading-[1.05] font-semibold tracking-[-0.038em]">
      Privacy
    </h1>
    <p class="mono mt-3 text-fg-muted">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <div
      class="mt-12 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-fg-dim
             [&_h2]:mt-12 [&_h2]:mb-4 [&_h2]:font-display [&_h2]:text-[22px] [&_h2]:font-semibold [&_h2]:tracking-[-0.01em] [&_h2]:text-fg
             [&_p]:mt-4
             [&_ul]:mt-4 [&_ul]:list-disc [&_ul]:space-y-2 [&_ul]:pl-5 [&_ul]:marker:text-fg-muted
             [&_strong]:font-medium [&_strong]:text-fg
             [&_code]:font-mono [&_code]:text-[0.92em] [&_code]:text-fg
             [&_a:not([class])]:text-fg [&_a:not([class])]:underline [&_a:not([class])]:decoration-rule-2 [&_a:not([class])]:underline-offset-4"
    >
      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <p>
        {APP_NAME} runs entirely on your Mac. It has no account, no telemetry, and
        <strong>no server of ours to talk to</strong> — there is nothing in it that makes a network
        request. We never see your mail, your messages, your contacts, your calendar, your notes,
        your reminders, your browsing history or your places.
      </p>

      <h2>The part that actually matters</h2>
      <p>
        The interesting question is not what we collect. It is <strong>where your data goes once
        {APP_NAME} reads it</strong>, and the answer is: to whichever agent you connected.
      </p>
      <p>
        {APP_NAME} is an MCP server. It reads from the Apple apps on your Mac and hands the results
        to the MCP client you configured — Claude Desktop, Claude Code, or anything else that speaks
        the protocol. <strong>If that client is a cloud service, then the mail, messages and
        contacts it asked for are sent to that service</strong>, under that company's privacy policy
        and not ours. {APP_NAME} does not do the sending, but it is what makes the data available to
        be sent, and pretending otherwise would be the most misleading thing this page could do.
      </p>
      <p>
        Install this deliberately. It is exactly as private as the agent you point it at.
      </p>

      <h2>What it can reach</h2>
      <p>
        Only what macOS has granted, and macOS asks you per app. {APP_NAME} reads through the same
        permission system anything else on your Mac uses — Automation and Full Disk Access — and a
        surface it has not been granted returns an error rather than data.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Read</strong> across Mail, Messages, Contacts, Calendar, Notes, Reminders, Safari and Maps.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Writes are off by default, per surface.</strong> The toggle decides whether the
          mutating tools are registered at all, so an agent with writes off cannot see they exist —
          it is not a refusal it could argue with.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What it stores</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing of yours. {APP_NAME} keeps no copy of anything it reads: it queries at the moment a
        tool is called and passes the result on. There is no index, no cache of message bodies, and
        no database of your contacts.
      </p>

      <h2>Checking rather than trusting</h2>
      <p>
        A privacy policy is a promise. This one is checkable, because
        <a href={REPO_URL} rel="noopener noreferrer">the source is public</a>. If you want to
        confirm the claim at the top of this page, search the tree for outbound HTTP — there is
        none, which is a thing you can verify in a minute rather than take our word for.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about any of this belong on
        <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues`} rel="noopener noreferrer">the issue tracker</a>, where the
        answer is visible to the next person asking. For anything you would rather not post
        publicly, email <a href="mailto:support@mgcrea.io">support@mgcrea.io</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes materially, the change lands in the repository's history like
        everything else, and the date at the top of this page moves. The canonical copy lives at
        <a href={`${SITE_URL}/privacy`}>{SITE_URL}/privacy</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
